From 84f5fdce0afd3d5e8737d0b1e7dd1f8bae11c73a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Antoine Pichot <antoine.pichot@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 20:44:55 +0900
Subject: [PATCH v2 1/9] media: i2c: ov7251: Use in-range PLL1 dividers for the
 319.2 MHz link

The 19.2 MHz PLL1 table for the 319.2 MHz link frequency divides after
multiplying. Its pre-divider (register 0x30B4, bits [3:0], a coded
field where 0x01 means divide by 1) is 0x01 and its divider (register
0x30B1, bits [4:0], a literal divisor from 1 to 16) is 0x04, so with
the multiplier of 0x85 (133) the PLL1 multiplier output, the VCO of
figure 2-12 of the OV7750/OV7251 datasheet (rev 2.12), runs at
19.2 x 133 = 2553.6 MHz before the divider brings it down to 638.4 MHz.
Table 2-11 limits the multiplier output to 300 to 800 MHz: the table
runs the oscillator at 3.2 times the datasheet maximum.

Divide before multiplying instead: pre-divider 0x04 (divide by 4, a
4.8 MHz reference, within the 4 to 27 MHz input range) and divider
0x01. The multiplier output is then 4.8 x 133 = 638.4 MHz, inside the
allowed range, and the MIPI clock after the unchanged MIPI divider
(0x30B5 = 0x05, divide by 1) is the same 638.4 Mbps, so the 319.2 MHz
link frequency does not change. The Intel ov7251 Windows driver
(30.18305.6.5127) programs this arrangement for its Windows Hello 30
fps mode on the Surface Book 2.

On ACPI systems, where ipu-bridge lists a single link frequency, this
table has been unreachable since it was added: ov7251_check_hwcfg()
stores the wrong index and always selects the 240 MHz table. The next
patch fixes the index and makes this table live, so it must not be
applied without this one.

On a Surface Pro 7+ (IPU6), with the index fix alone, 7 of 10
150-frame runs at 30 fps had CSI-2 errors and 3 of them stalled. With
this change 0 of 13 did: every run delivered 150 of 150 frames at
30.00 fps with no dropped buffers and no CSI-2 error line, and an i2c
readback mid-stream showed only 0x30B1 and 0x30B4 changed. A Surface
Book 2 (IPU3) streams clean with either table, 3 runs of 155 frames
each. On a Raspberry Pi with a 24 MHz clock, Dave Stevenson measured
with an oscilloscope that the out-of-range tables do not produce their
nominal frequency, and proposed these values independently.

The 24 MHz tables have problems of their own (the 240 MHz one runs at
400 MHz and the 319.2 MHz one has its VCO at 2128 MHz) and are left
alone here, as nobody in the thread can test a change to them.

Fixes: ed9566ce1946 ("media: i2c: Add support for new frequencies to ov7251")
Cc: stable@vger.kernel.org
Link: https://lore.kernel.org/linux-media/6ae0e944.b6db0abd.16b13d.d261@mx.google.com/
Link: https://lore.kernel.org/linux-media/CAPY8ntBFJJPg3y46gC=bNq_55QifC7mKmeq8+pAEDVN42fGUXw@mail.gmail.com/
Signed-off-by: Antoine Pichot <antoine.pichot@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/media/i2c/ov7251.c | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/media/i2c/ov7251.c b/drivers/media/i2c/ov7251.c
index fce6344b5120..f429de5f1f7c 100644
--- a/drivers/media/i2c/ov7251.c
+++ b/drivers/media/i2c/ov7251.c
@@ -174,9 +174,9 @@ static const struct ov7251_pll1_cfg ov7251_pll1_cfg_19_2_mhz_240_mhz = {
 };
 
 static const struct ov7251_pll1_cfg ov7251_pll1_cfg_19_2_mhz_319_2_mhz = {
-	.pre_div = 0x01,
+	.pre_div = 0x04,
 	.mult = 0x85,
-	.div = 0x04,
+	.div = 0x01,
 	.pix_div = 0x0a,
 	.mipi_div = 0x05,
 };
